from pathlib import Path
import json, datetime, hashlib
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
OUT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
edits=[]
def change(name,old,new,why):
    p=ROOT/'content/chapters'/name
    s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
    backup=OUT/(name+'.before')
    if not backup.exists(): backup.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
    assert s.count(old)==1,(name,old[:90],s.count(old))
    p.write_text(s.replace(old,new),encoding='utf-8',newline='\n')
    edits.append(dict(file=name,old=old,new=new,reason=why))
def add_after(name,anchor,extra,why): change(name,anchor,anchor+'\n\n'+extra,why)
def fold(name,start,end,title):
    p=ROOT/'content/chapters'/name
    s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
    a=s.index(start); b=s.index(end,a)
    body=s[a:b].rstrip()
    change(name,body,'::: {.publisher-clarification-note .publisher-fold-always title="'+title+'"}\n\n'+body+'\n\n:::', 'Fold formal mathematics while retaining its interpretation in the main reading path.')

add_after('01b-persistence.md','## Stable activity with a vulnerable stored value {#sec:FOUNDATIONS-ATTRACTOR}',r'''Imagine a group of neurons maintaining a pattern that represents an eye angle after a movement command has ended. Random spikes disturb that pattern. Recurrent input can correct some disturbances by changing the cells' firing back toward a persistent pattern. But if a disturbance produces the pattern for a different angle, an ideal memory circuit has no record of which angle was originally intended. [Keeping a pattern stable is therefore different from keeping the stored value correct.]{.key-statement}''','Establish the physical memory problem before dynamical definitions.')
anchor='A line attractor has one represented coordinate; a ring can represent a periodic coordinate such as heading.[^f-memory]'
add_after('01b-persistence.md',anchor,r'''In the illustrative two-cell model below, the difference between the firing rates represents the angle. Raising both rates equally leaves that difference unchanged; the model's feedback removes the common increase. Raising one rate while lowering the other changes the represented angle. That second change persists. The calculation makes these two responses explicit; it is not a fitted biological circuit.''','Keep the essential two-disturbance contrast visible outside the example.')
add_after('01b-persistence.md','The second equation retains any angle set by the initial state.',r'''Increasing $a$ shortens the recovery time $1/a$ for the common activity disturbance. It does not correct the angle error, because $\dot\theta$ is still zero. At fixed rate disturbance, a larger tuning slope $k$ gives a smaller decoded angle change through division by $2k$; changing $k$ in an actual circuit may also change its dynamics.''','Explain parameter changes in the existing numerical model.')
change('01b-persistence.md',r'If persistent angular changes arrive repeatedly, their errors accumulate. Divide time into intervals of length $\Delta t$.',r'''Each extra or missing spike changes synaptic input. After the circuit has corrected the disturbances it can remove, a small change in the represented angle may remain. Repeating this process makes trials that began at the same angle gradually spread apart. We first model these lasting changes as independent, with no preferred direction and a constant variance per unit time. These are assumptions about the surviving changes, not about complete spike trains.

Divide time into intervals of length $\Delta t$.''','Link random steps to retained neural disturbances and state the approximation before its algebra.')
fold('01b-persistence.md','Divide time into intervals of length','[The parameter $D$ is the diffusion coefficient.','Mathematical model: independent steps and Brownian motion')
change('01b-persistence.md','[The parameter $D$ is the diffusion coefficient. It measures the rate of variance growth; a single trajectory need not move monotonically away from its starting value.]{.key-statement}',r'''[The **diffusion coefficient** $D$ measures half the rate at which the variance of the stored angle grows.]{.definition-explanation} With independent, zero-mean increments of constant variance per unit time,

$$
\operatorname{Var}[\theta(t)-\theta(0)]=2Dt.
$$

Here $D$ has units degrees squared per second when $\theta$ is in degrees. More frequent retained disturbances, or larger ones at the same frequency, increase $D$. At a fixed delay, doubling $D$ doubles error variance and multiplies its standard deviation by $\sqrt{2}$. [A single memory trajectory can turn back even while the spread across trials grows.]{.key-statement} The [network calculation](ch-memory-dynamics.html#sec-memory-diffusion) obtains $D$ from the spike variability and the lasting effect of each cell's disturbance.''','Give the spreading parameter a visible neural cause and quantitative consequence.')
fold('01b-persistence.md','Two times along a Brownian path are correlated','A finite range, a periodic coordinate','Mathematical detail: why two times share accumulated noise')
add_after('01b-persistence.md',':::\n\nA finite range, a periodic coordinate',r'''TEMP''','TEMP') if False else None
# Keep the reason for the covariance fold visible without needing its proof.
change('01b-persistence.md','::: {.publisher-clarification-note .publisher-fold-always title="Mathematical detail: why two times share accumulated noise"}',r'''A later memory state contains earlier accumulated errors as well as new ones. Observing it twice therefore does not give two independent copies of the original memory. The covariance calculation below quantifies the shared history used in the readout chapter.

::: {.publisher-clarification-note .publisher-fold-always title="Mathematical detail: why two times share accumulated noise"}''','Keep physical meaning visible before the covariance proof.')

add_after('02-memory-dynamics.md','The paper allows general connectivity and transfer functions at first ([Model, Eqs. 7–9, p. 5](../papers/burak-fiete-2012-persistent-activity.pdf#page=5&zoom=125,0,372)).[^p1-model]',r'''A spike from cell $j$ raises $s_j$ by one and therefore changes the input to cell $i$ by $W_{ij}$. That contribution then decays over time $\tau$. For an increasing transfer function, a positive weight raises the receiving cell's rate and a negative weight lowers it. The resulting new spikes affect other cells in turn. Thus the weights set where a disturbance goes, the transfer function sets the rate response, and $\tau$ sets how long each synaptic contribution lasts. The input $g_i$ is in the model's input units; it is not specified as a measured membrane voltage.''','Complete the spike-to-input-to-rate causal chain.')
add_after('02-memory-dynamics.md','The entries of $K$ have units of inverse time.[^p1-projection]',r'''The term $-\delta_{ij}/\tau$ removes a disturbance as synaptic activation fades. The term $\phi'(\bar g_i)W_{ij}$ describes recurrent renewal: the connection converts a change in cell $j$'s activation into input to cell $i$, and the local slope of the transfer function converts that input into a rate change. A steeper slope makes this response stronger. [Whether a disturbance decays, persists, or grows depends on the combined feedback through the whole network.]{.key-statement} A stronger connection alone does not establish better memory.''','Read the two matrix terms as decay versus recurrent renewal.')

add_after('02b-memory-long-delays.md',r'Now $\mathbf u=(1,1)^T$, $\mathbf v=(1/2,1/2)^T$.',r'''Here $\theta$ is an activation coordinate, not an angle, and both steady rates are positive only when $\theta>|b_0|/2$. When both activations rise together, each cell raises the other's firing enough to replace the extra synaptic decay. This common increase persists and changes the stored value. A disturbance in their difference instead decays toward the difference set by the biases. Larger $\theta$ raises both firing rates and hence both Poisson count variances. Because the projection weights stay fixed in this example, more of that variability remains as memory error.''','Explain mutual excitation and why the noise changes with the stored activation.')

add_after('03-memory-information.md',r'Maintaining those curves requires retuning the network, because $\bar s=\tau\bar r$.',r'''At fixed rates, longer-lasting spike effects give larger mean activations. The activation difference between two nearby stored values also grows in proportion to $\tau$. In this symmetric comparison, one spike's unit activation change therefore produces a smaller displacement of the stored value: the projection weights scale as $1/\tau$. The spike variance per unit time stays fixed, so the retained variance scales as $1/\tau^2$.''','Explain why slower synaptic decay changes diffusion in the controlled comparison.')
# The preceding insertion was inside a paragraph; put the contrasting comparison in its own paragraph.
change('03-memory-information.md',r'The spike variance per unit time stays fixed, so the retained variance scales as $1/\tau^2$. In contrast,',r'The spike variance per unit time stays fixed, so the retained variance scales as $1/\tau^2$.'+'\n\nIn contrast,','Separate the two different controlled comparisons.')

