add_after('04-eye-drift.md',r"These are the paired signs in the paper's Eq. 7, with $\tau_+>\tau_-$ when $k,r,m>0$ and $r^2>4km$.",r'''At rest, the inverse equation gives $x=f/k$: with the threshold fixed, doubling $k$ halves the position offset predicted from the same steady firing rate. During a change, $r$ and $m$ also affect the response through the two timescales. This is a consequence of the fitted relationship, not evidence that changing one recorded neuron's rate alone sets eye position.''','Interpret fitted parameters without reversing a descriptive fit into a single-cell causal claim.')
add_after('04-eye-drift.md','Multiplying by position, velocity, and acceleration gives spikes/s in all three terms.',r'''The position term describes ongoing firing associated with holding the eye at an angle. The velocity term allows a different rate while the eye passes through that same angle; the acceleration term allows another change while its velocity changes. Thus $k$, $r$, and $m$ describe different demands on the motor signal. They are fitted coefficients of the combined neural and mechanical relationship. In particular, $m$ is not a direct measurement of the eye's mass.''','Explain why position, velocity and acceleration affect the motor signal differently.')

add_after('04b-eye-timing.md',r'\tau\dot E+E=B,\qquad dB=\sqrt{2D}\,dW.'+'\n$$',r'''Here $B$ is the changing position command and $E$ is the eye angle that follows it, both in degrees. The mismatch $B-E$ drives velocity: a command to the right of the current position makes the eye move right. For the same mismatch, a larger response time $\tau$ gives a smaller velocity, so the eye takes longer to catch up and smooths rapid command changes more strongly. This response time belongs to the simplified eye filter; it is not automatically the synaptic decay time used in the memory model.''','Translate the filter equation into a physical command-response mechanism.')
add_after('04b-eye-timing.md','which grows linearly rather than approaching a plateau.',r'''Holding the internal diffusion coefficient $D$ fixed, increasing $\tau$ reduces short-lag displacement and moves the transition to linear growth to longer lags. It does not stop accumulating error: the long-lag MSD growth rate still approaches $2D$. Increasing $D$ instead raises the entire MSD curve in proportion, at fixed $\tau$.''','Separate input noise strength from response speed in the observable curve.')

add_after('05-place-fields.md',r"In the paper's Equation 3 the firing rate is proportional to $[h-q\sigma_h]_+$, which is equivalent to this form after choosing the gain.[^p3-model]",r'''The input threshold summarizes how much combined input is needed before the model cell fires. It can reflect the cell's own response properties and inhibitory input. Raising $q$ at a fixed input map leaves fewer positions active. If the absolute threshold above the input mean is positive and held fixed, increasing the input standard deviation $\sigma_h$ lowers $q$, making activity more widespread. Changing $a$ instead changes rates within active regions while preserving their zero-rate boundaries. A fixed positive detection cutoff in Hz would change this last conclusion. [A fitted threshold describes the balance of input and response; it does not identify which biological quantity changed.]{.key-statement}''','Map normalized threshold and firing gain to different biological or measurement changes.')
# Formal random-function definition is available, but the map's role is visible first.
fold('05-place-fields.md','[A [**Gaussian process**]','Three assumptions have different jobs:','Mathematical definitions: Gaussian processes and spatial covariance')
change('05-place-fields.md','::: {.publisher-clarification-note .publisher-fold-always title="Mathematical definitions: Gaussian processes and spatial covariance"}',r'''The model assigns a random input value to each position, with nearby values linked so that a cell can have a continuous active region. A **Gaussian process** specifies a distribution of such maps. Its mean sets the average input, and its spatial covariance describes how inputs at two positions vary together across possible maps. The chosen covariance must also make the maps smooth enough for the derivatives used below. After a map is drawn, moving the animal changes which position is evaluated, not the map itself.

::: {.publisher-clarification-note .publisher-fold-always title="Mathematical definitions: Gaussian processes and spatial covariance"}''','Keep the physical map and covariance roles visible ahead of the formal definition.')

add_after('05c-place-tests.md','The statistical model becomes biologically useful if it suggests how a circuit could make the maps. A random weighted sum of many inputs is one possible mechanism, and the supplement makes its assumptions explicit.',r'''At position $x$, each input cell contributes according to its spatial firing profile. Fixed connection weights determine how those contributions combine at the receiving cell. The source treats the sum as effective input relative to a background that can be balanced through inhibition or regulation of excitation and inhibition. The signed random weights below are part of this simplified input model; they do not establish the signs of individual biological synapses.''','Explain the physical objects and the scope of signed random weights before summation.')
add_after('05c-place-tests.md',r'&=v_W\sum_i u_i(x)u_i(y).\end{aligned}' if False else r'&=v_W\sum_i u_i(x)u_i(y).'+'\n\\end{aligned}\n$$',r'''The same fixed weight multiplies an input cell's activity at both positions. For positive input profiles, cells active at both $x$ and $y$ therefore make positive contributions to covariance across possible weight choices. Little overlap gives a smaller contribution. For profiles defined as deviations from background, the products can also be negative. Broad input profiles can produce broad spatial covariance, as the Gaussian example below shows.''','Interpret spatial covariance as shared weighted spatial input, retaining sign qualifications.')
add_after('05c-place-tests.md','For input profiles with different shapes, average the last integral over the distribution of those shapes, as in Methods S2, Equation S8.',r'''At fixed profile shape, increasing the weight variance $v_W$ or input density $\rho$ increases the covariance amplitude. It does not change the normalized spatial dependence in this construction. Widening each input profile instead broadens the distances over which positions share input. These are different changes: input amplitude can alter the effective threshold $q$ when the absolute threshold is held fixed, whereas input width changes the spatial scale.''','Separate input strength and density from spatial width in the model parameters.')

